  "query_preview": "..\nThrough Friday...\n\nA shortwave lifting north and east through the region is bringing\nisolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms to northern and\neastern areas early this morning. Expect showers and \nthunderstorms to gradually diminish over the next few hours, with \ndry conditions prevailing through the remainder of the night.\n\nThe main focus through the forecast period continues to be the\nvery hot conditions this weekend through Monday as a strong ridge\nof high pressure builds over the region (500mb heights of 596-600\ndm Sunday). There remains high confidence in temperatures of 95 \nto 110 degrees, warmest on Sunday with very warm temperatures \naloft (700mb temperatures near or exceeding 20C and 850 mb \ntemperatures of 35-40C). Lower river valley locations east of\nBillings continue to have the best chance at exceeding 110\ndegrees. Temperatures this warm could threaten all-time record \nhigh temperatures at all climate sites on Sunday. Along with the \nhot daytime temperatures, overnight low temperatures in the 60s \nand 70s, warmest Sunday night, will exacerbate the risk of heat \nstress. An Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect for Saturday\nthrough Sunday evening, though it may need to be extended through\nMonday for some areas.\n\nVery dry conditions will accompany the heat, with relative \nhumidity values decreasing into the single digits to teens percent\ntoday and Sunday, and teens to 20s percent Monday. Breezy south \nto southwest winds develop over the area (gusts 20s to 30s mph) \nduring the day on Sunday, ahead of a cold front from the northwest\nSunday evening/night. The combination of breezy winds, very hot \ntemperatures, and very dry conditions will lead to elevated to\ncritical fire weather concerns. A Fire Weather Watch is in effect\non Sunday for much of the area. Take care not to cause a spark. \n\nEnsembles continue to shift the ridge east a bit by midweek \nallowing temperatures to cool slightly (highs in the upper 80s and\n90s), and monsoonal moisture to move in from the southwest. This \nwould result in a daily chance of showers/thunderstorms for the \narea along with higher relative humidity values. Ensembles suggest\nremarkable PWATs reaching up to 1.50 inches for much of the area \nfrom Tuesday onward. Any decent impulse in the monsoonal flow may \nresult in storms with very heavy rainfall. We will need to monitor\nthose PWAT trends closely heading into next week. \n\nBelow are the all-time record high temperatures at each of our \nclimate stations:\n\nBillings, MT    108F\nLivingston, MT  105F\nMiles City, MT  111F\nSheridan, WY    107F\nBaker, MT       109F\n\nSTP",
